// A forbid that denies nothing, because nothing was ever going to be allowed.
//
// `Approve` has no permit, so it is denied by default. The forbid below then changes no verdict
// in any session: delete it and every decision comes out the same. It reads like a control and is
// not one.
//
//     python tests/strands/vacuity.py tests/policies/dead_forbid.dw
//     ==> forbid #2  action == Approve   DEAD   deleting it changes no verdict in any session
//
// Worth separating from the tidiness argument. A dead forbid is not merely surplus: somebody wrote
// it believing they were closing something, and the thing they meant to close was already shut for
// an unrelated reason. If the permit it was guarding against is ever added, the forbid starts
// mattering — and nobody will connect the two changes.

permit (
    principal,
    action == Anchor::Action::"Trade",
    resource
);

forbid (
    principal,
    action == Anchor::Action::"Approve",
    resource
);
